1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,153
In Washington State, charging an electric car at home instead of buying gas saves about one thousand one hundred ninety-eight dollars a year.

2
00:00:09,453 --> 00:00:12,768
In Massachusetts, it saves about two hundred five.

3
00:00:13,068 --> 00:00:13,961
Same car.

4
00:00:14,261 --> 00:00:15,205
Same miles.

5
00:00:15,505 --> 00:00:16,681
So what's going on?

6
00:00:16,981 --> 00:00:27,527
In this video, I'll work out what it really costs to drive an electric car charged at home, compared with a gas car, for the whole country and for nine states.

7
00:00:27,827 --> 00:00:36,098
I'll use official numbers for how far Americans drive, how efficient the cars are, and what electricity and gasoline cost.

8
00:00:36,398 --> 00:00:40,599
And at the end, I'll give you a two-line formula to check your own state.

9
00:00:40,899 --> 00:00:42,319
First, the ingredients.

10
00:00:42,619 --> 00:00:43,768
There are only four.

11
00:00:44,068 --> 00:00:45,574
One: how far you drive.

12
00:00:45,874 --> 00:00:54,580
According to the Federal Highway Administration, the average car in America covers about eleven thousand twenty-six miles a year.

13
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:56,863
Two: how efficient a gas car is.

14
00:00:57,163 --> 00:01:07,167
The EPA says the average new vehicle from model year twenty twenty-four gets about twenty-seven point two miles per gallon in real-world driving.

15
00:01:07,467 --> 00:01:08,663
That's a record high.

16
00:01:08,963 --> 00:01:11,476
Three: how efficient an electric car is.

17
00:01:11,776 --> 00:01:20,639
Popular twenty twenty-five models are rated by the EPA at between twenty-seven and thirty-one kilowatt-hours per hundred miles.

18
00:01:20,939 --> 00:01:31,152
For example, the Tesla Model Y Long Range rear-wheel drive is rated at twenty-seven, and the Chevrolet Equinox EV front-wheel drive at thirty-one.

19
00:01:31,452 --> 00:01:33,411
I'll use thirty, a middle value.

20
00:01:33,711 --> 00:01:35,711
And four: what you pay for energy.

21
00:01:36,011 --> 00:01:49,359
Nationally, regular gasoline averaged about three dollars and ten cents a gallon in twenty twenty-five, and home electricity is about eighteen point two cents per kilowatt-hour in twenty twenty-six.

22
00:01:49,659 --> 00:01:51,117
That's everything we need.

23
00:01:51,417 --> 00:01:54,160
Let's start small, with every hundred miles.

24
00:01:54,460 --> 00:02:02,416
A gas car at twenty-seven point two miles per gallon needs about three point seven gallons to go a hundred miles.

25
00:02:02,716 --> 00:02:06,976
At the national price, that's about eleven dollars and thirty-nine cents.

26
00:02:07,276 --> 00:02:18,734
An electric car at thirty kilowatt-hours per hundred miles, at the national electricity price, costs about five dollars and forty-six cents for the same hundred miles.

27
00:02:19,034 --> 00:02:23,539
So charging at home costs less than half as much per mile, on average.

28
00:02:23,839 --> 00:02:26,302
Now scale that up to a full year of driving.

29
00:02:26,602 --> 00:02:36,886
At eleven thousand twenty-six miles, the gas car burns about four hundred five gallons, which costs about one thousand two hundred fifty-five dollars.

30
00:02:37,186 --> 00:02:44,979
The electric car uses about three thousand three hundred eight kilowatt-hours, which costs about six hundred two dollars.

31
00:02:45,279 --> 00:02:50,793
That's a difference of about six hundred fifty-four dollars a year, on national averages.

32
00:02:51,093 --> 00:02:53,509
Before going further, here's a cautious check.

33
00:02:53,809 --> 00:02:57,529
Charging a battery always wastes a bit of energy as heat.

34
00:02:57,829 --> 00:03:05,136
Oak Ridge National Laboratory says charging efficiency is often between eighty-four and ninety-three percent.

35
00:03:05,436 --> 00:03:17,594
The government's fuel economy site defines the EPA number as the electricity the car uses to travel a hundred miles, but it doesn't spell out how charging losses are counted.

36
00:03:17,894 --> 00:03:19,524
So let's take the worst case.

37
00:03:19,824 --> 00:03:29,402
If we add charging losses on top, at eighty-four percent efficiency, the electric car would cost about seven hundred sixteen dollars a year.

38
00:03:29,702 --> 00:03:32,859
It still saves about five hundred thirty-nine dollars.

39
00:03:33,159 --> 00:03:34,878
So the result holds either way.

40
00:03:35,178 --> 00:03:37,431
But nobody pays the national average.

41
00:03:37,731 --> 00:03:39,231
Let's go state by state.

42
00:03:39,531 --> 00:03:49,031
Washington has relatively cheap electricity, about fourteen point four cents, and expensive gas, about four dollars and thirteen cents.

43
00:03:49,331 --> 00:03:51,975
That's the best combination for an electric car.

44
00:03:52,275 --> 00:04:00,211
Gas costs about one thousand six hundred seventy-five dollars a year, and charging at home about four hundred seventy-seven.

45
00:04:00,511 --> 00:04:04,238
That saves about one thousand one hundred ninety-eight dollars.

46
00:04:04,538 --> 00:04:12,743
In Florida, gas costs about one thousand one hundred ninety-nine dollars a year, and electricity about five hundred seven.

47
00:04:13,043 --> 00:04:15,351
That saves about six hundred ninety-two.

48
00:04:15,651 --> 00:04:21,623
Minnesota lands close to the national picture, saving about six hundred fifty-seven dollars.

49
00:04:21,923 --> 00:04:24,500
Colorado saves about six hundred seven.

50
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:30,159
Texas has the cheapest gas of these states, about two dollars and sixty-six cents.

51
00:04:30,459 --> 00:04:35,602
Even so, an electric car saves about five hundred forty-eight dollars a year.

52
00:04:35,902 --> 00:04:38,602
Ohio saves about five hundred seventy-eight.

53
00:04:38,902 --> 00:04:41,168
California is the interesting one.

54
00:04:41,468 --> 00:04:46,556
Its electricity is the most expensive here, at about thirty-two point six cents.

55
00:04:46,856 --> 00:04:52,024
But its gas is also very expensive, about four dollars and forty-one cents.

56
00:04:52,324 --> 00:04:56,788
So the electric car still saves about seven hundred ten dollars a year.

57
00:04:57,088 --> 00:04:58,990
Now the two states where it gets tight.

58
00:04:59,290 --> 00:05:07,668
In New York, with electricity near twenty-nine point three cents, the savings drop to about two hundred seventy-one dollars a year.

59
00:05:07,968 --> 00:05:14,867
And in Massachusetts, with electricity near thirty point two cents, it's about two hundred five dollars.

60
00:05:15,167 --> 00:05:21,937
In the worst-case check with charging losses added, Massachusetts drops to about fifteen dollars a year.

61
00:05:22,237 --> 00:05:23,459
Basically a tie.

62
00:05:23,759 --> 00:05:28,784
Here's another way to see it: what an electric car adds to your home's electric bill.

63
00:05:29,084 --> 00:05:36,686
At eleven thousand twenty-six miles a year, charging at home adds about three thousand three hundred eight kilowatt-hours.

64
00:05:36,986 --> 00:05:43,558
The average American home uses about ten thousand seven hundred ninety-one kilowatt-hours a year.

65
00:05:43,858 --> 00:05:48,557
So the car adds about thirty-one percent to the household's electricity use.

66
00:05:48,857 --> 00:05:56,284
Month by month, that's about two hundred seventy-six kilowatt-hours, or about fifty dollars, at the national price.

67
00:05:56,584 --> 00:06:00,365
And the government's household survey has a real-world number too.

68
00:06:00,665 --> 00:06:08,380
Homes that charge an electric car used about two thousand three hundred sixty-three kilowatt-hours a year for charging.

69
00:06:08,680 --> 00:06:12,304
At today's price, that's about four hundred thirty dollars.

70
00:06:12,604 --> 00:06:19,553
That's below our estimate, a reminder that real households vary in how much they drive and where they plug in.

71
00:06:19,853 --> 00:06:22,116
One more thing: both prices move.

72
00:06:22,416 --> 00:06:28,092
In twenty twenty, regular gas averaged about two dollars and seventeen cents a gallon.

73
00:06:28,392 --> 00:06:33,071
In twenty twenty-two, it spiked to about three dollars and ninety-five cents.

74
00:06:33,371 --> 00:06:37,794
In twenty twenty-five, it was back to about three dollars and ten cents.

75
00:06:38,094 --> 00:06:46,888
Home electricity rose more steadily, from about thirteen point two cents in twenty twenty to about eighteen point two cents today.

76
00:06:47,188 --> 00:06:59,176
Per hundred miles, gas went from about seven dollars and ninety-seven cents in twenty twenty, to about fourteen fifty-three at the twenty twenty-two peak, and about eleven thirty-nine now.

77
00:06:59,476 --> 00:07:03,990
Electricity went from about three ninety-four to about five forty-six.

78
00:07:04,290 --> 00:07:10,478
In twenty twenty, and again today, charging at home cost about half as much per mile as gas.

79
00:07:10,778 --> 00:07:21,695
At the twenty twenty-two peak, with electricity at about fifteen cents, it cost about four dollars and fifty-one cents per hundred miles, less than a third of gas.

80
00:07:21,995 --> 00:07:24,946
Gas simply swings a lot more than electricity.

81
00:07:25,246 --> 00:07:26,565
So here's the pattern.

82
00:07:26,865 --> 00:07:30,814
The savings depend on two prices: gas and electricity.

83
00:07:31,114 --> 00:07:35,320
Cheap power and expensive gas, like Washington, is the jackpot.

84
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:40,034
Expensive power and average gas, like Massachusetts, shrinks the gap.

85
00:07:40,334 --> 00:07:49,616
On national gas prices, electricity would have to cost about thirty-eight cents per kilowatt-hour before charging at home stopped being cheaper.

86
00:07:49,916 --> 00:07:56,407
In the worst case, with charging losses added, that line drops to about thirty-one point nine cents.

87
00:07:56,707 --> 00:07:58,758
Most states are far below that line.

88
00:07:59,058 --> 00:08:02,587
A few in the Northeast and California are getting close.

89
00:08:02,887 --> 00:08:05,394
Now let's do your own state, in two lines.

90
00:08:05,694 --> 00:08:13,643
Line one, the electric car: multiply its rating in kilowatt-hours per hundred miles by your electricity price.

91
00:08:13,943 --> 00:08:20,647
Thirty times eighteen point two cents is about five dollars and forty-six cents per hundred miles.

92
00:08:20,947 --> 00:08:28,338
Line two, the gas car: divide one hundred by its miles per gallon, then multiply by your gas price.

93
00:08:28,638 --> 00:08:37,319
One hundred divided by twenty-seven point two, times three dollars and ten cents, is about eleven dollars and thirty-nine cents.

94
00:08:37,619 --> 00:08:43,240
The difference, times the miles you drive divided by a hundred, is your yearly savings.

95
00:08:43,540 --> 00:08:45,147
And the car itself matters.

96
00:08:45,447 --> 00:08:58,009
A Tesla Model Y Long Range rear-wheel drive, at twenty-seven kilowatt-hours per hundred miles, costs about five hundred forty-two dollars a year to charge at the national price.

97
00:08:58,309 --> 00:09:03,705
An Equinox EV front-wheel drive, at thirty-one, about six hundred twenty-two.

98
00:09:04,005 --> 00:09:06,281
A few things are not in these numbers.

99
00:09:06,581 --> 00:09:08,272
This is home charging only.

100
00:09:08,572 --> 00:09:12,625
Public charging prices vary a lot, and they're not included here.

101
00:09:12,925 --> 00:09:17,032
It also leaves out the price of the car, insurance and maintenance.

102
00:09:17,332 --> 00:09:19,802
This is purely the cost of energy to drive.

103
00:09:20,102 --> 00:09:21,507
And these are averages.

104
00:09:21,807 --> 00:09:27,150
If you drive more than eleven thousand miles a year, the savings grow with every mile.

105
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:31,705
So, is charging an electric car at home cheaper than buying gas?

106
00:09:32,005 --> 00:09:37,113
On national averages, yes, by about six hundred fifty-four dollars a year.

107
00:09:37,413 --> 00:09:41,294
In Washington, by about one thousand one hundred ninety-eight.

108
00:09:41,594 --> 00:09:47,033
In Massachusetts, only about two hundred five, and close to a tie in the worst case.

109
00:09:47,333 --> 00:09:51,883
Check your own two prices with the formula, and you'll know where your state lands.

110
00:09:52,183 --> 00:10:00,157
And if you're wondering what else charging adds to your home's electric bill, my ranking of fifteen home appliances is on this channel.
